a third case of coronavirus the patient  being treated here and a specialist unit  inside st. Thomas's Hospital in London  it's no surprise the virus is fast  spreading and more cases here in the UK  were expected but this time there is a  significant development they acquired it  in Asia but not in China and at the  moment the great majority of the cases  in the world are still in China the  great majority there are a few in other  countries and our view is because of  that is probably now prudent to slightly  widen the geographical area where  firstly doctors in the UK in the NHS  know that they should test people the  patient's identity is being closely  protected we know very little about  exactly where and how they came into  contact with the virus but we do know  one thing that there's no connection  between this latest case and the other  two known cases a student from York  University and a family member were  identified last week after staying at  this hotel they are known to have  contracted the virus in China  now the advice to NHS doctors and all  frontline staff will have to be updated  in the past they've only had to worry  about people who have travelled from  Wuhan  in China or who have been in contact  with people who have traveled to that  region now they'll have a much wider  geographical region to deal with the  analyst now includes China Thailand  Japan the Republic of Korea Hong Kong  Taiwan Singapore Malaysia and Macau the  Chinese are sensitive to criticism  accusations that they're withholding  information or are downplaying the  number of infected and dead China's  ambassador to Britain urged Western  countries to stop creating unnecessary  panic hope that the governments of all  countries including the UK should  understand and support China's efforts  respect the professional advice of top  new h:o  China is sharing more information than  it did during the deadly 2003 SARS  outbreak but it's not officially  confirming the death of dr. Lee wen  Liang who was one of the first to warn  of the dangers of this outbreak at the  end of December he was silenced by  authorities and forced to return to work  where he caught the virus from a patient  his death adding to the number of  fatalities rising daily we don't know  the source of the outbreak we don't know  what its natural reservoir is and we  don't properly understand its  transmissibility or severity to defeat  this outbreak we need answers to all  those questions we are learning more  about the virus but much remains unknown  pictures like this of medics and hazmat  suits taking away a patient suspected of  having corona virus in York will do  little to ease nerves in the city and  elsewhere the patient later tested  negative now the government is stressing  once again that its plans are robust and  the country is fully prepared to deal  with this epidemic  Ashish Joshi Sky News
 

